 ## Overview
-Gradient-Based Interpolation by Sample Splatting is a real-time technique for reconstructing source radiance $L_x$ in inhomogeneous, single-scattering media by interpolating values and gradients from a sparse set of sample points [S1]. It solves the problem of severe rendering artifacts (e.g., in light shafts and volumetric shadows) that occur when standard interpolation methods, such as Radial Basis Functions (RBF), fail to capture sharp, high-frequency shading variations [S1]. Unlike RBF methods which assume smooth shading, this method uses first-order Taylor approximations weighted by distance, enabling accurate reconstruction of local and directional illumination effects with significantly lower relative error (4.3% vs 14.7% for RBF) at comparable performance levels [S1].
+Gradient-Based Interpolation by Sample Splatting is a real-time technique for reconstructing source radiance $L_x$ in inhomogeneous, single-scattering media from a sparse set of sample points [S1]. It solves the problem of severe rendering artifacts (such as broken light shafts and incorrect volumetric shadows) that occur when standard interpolation methods (like RBF) fail to capture high-frequency shading variations near light sources [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that rely solely on radiance values, this method uses both the radiance value and its spatial gradient at each sample point to construct a first-order Taylor approximation, significantly reducing relative error (4.3% vs 14.7% for RBF) while maintaining real-time performance [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method consists of three main phases: (1) computing source radiance and gradients at sparse sample points, (2) splatting these samples into a 3D volume texture to interpolate radiance, and (3) ray marching through the resulting volume to compute final image radiance.
+The method operates in three phases: (1) computing source radiance and gradients at sparse sample points, (2) splatting these samples into a 3D volume texture to interpolate radiance at all voxels, and (3) ray marching through the resulting volume texture to compute final image radiance [S1].
 
